* Generate an encrypted RSA private key
OpenSSL asks for a passphrase. Keep the private key and its passphrase private; the public key can be shared.
@code bash
umask 077
openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -aes-256-cbc -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:3072 -out private.pem
openssl pkey -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem
@end

* Encrypt a short file with the public key
Use an existing `secret-api-key.txt`. RSA handles only short inputs; this is not a general file-encryption recipe.
@code bash
openssl pkeyutl -encrypt -pubin -inkey public.pem -in secret-api-key.txt -out secret-api-key.enc -pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:oaep -pkeyopt rsa_oaep_md:sha256 -pkeyopt rsa_mgf1_md:sha256
@end

* Decrypt with the private key
@code bash
openssl pkeyutl -decrypt -inkey private.pem -in secret-api-key.enc -out decrypted.txt -pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:oaep -pkeyopt rsa_oaep_md:sha256 -pkeyopt rsa_mgf1_md:sha256
@end
Both sides must use the same OAEP settings. These commands replace the deprecated `rsautl` recipe; they do not decrypt its old output with different padding.

Related: {:encrypt-password-in-script:}[passphrase-based encryption]. Reference: {https://docs.openssl.org/3.0/man1/openssl-pkeyutl/}[pkeyutl].
